ulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d9a72d4-9e5c-5197-88ec-46bd4c7f840b" :question/math-academy-id "q-331892" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation, we get the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{{field-1}}=5{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ {{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}L{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)}=L{5{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ L{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)}=L{{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}sL{{x}_{1}(t)}-{x}_{1}(0)=5L{{x}_{1}(t)} \\\\ sL{{x}_{2}(t)}-{x}_{2}(0)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}-2L{{x}_{2}(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+3=5{X}_{1}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)+4={X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L{{x}_{1}(t)}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L{{x}_{2}(t)}$ are the Laplace transforms of the functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t),$ respectively."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd7b8fc8-26d3-5551-9a46-45725bd863a6" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Laplace Transform to a System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9bd1379-8e08-523c-bd87-ebf0872bee5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{X}_{1}(s)-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380262} {:answer/id #uuid "0cc6087f-f01a-5711-8deb-cc3eff7c3d0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{X}_{1}(s)-3" :db